STREAM kernel descriptor, version 1.

This file is the kernel. Its SHA-256 is the kernel's identity, and every log
names that hash on its first line. Changing one byte here makes a new kernel.

PREAMBLE
  The first line of a log is exactly: STREAM/1 kernel=sha256:<64 lowercase hex>
  This line never changes form under any kernel version.

ENTRY GRAMMAR (one entry per line, LF-terminated, UTF-8)
  entry   = time SP type SP object SP payload
  time    = 1*DIGIT [ "." 1*6DIGIT ]        ; unix seconds; fraction has no trailing zero
  type    = [ profile ":" ] verb [ phase ]
  profile = %x41-5A                          ; one uppercase letter
  verb    = one of the 64 names below
  phase   = "(" / ")" / "*"                  ; begin / end / sample; absent = instant
  object  = 1*( %x21-7E / UTF-8 non-ASCII )  ; no spaces or controls
  payload = canonical JSON object

CANONICAL JSON
  Objects: keys sorted by byte order, no duplicate keys. No whitespace anywhere.
  Numbers: integers only, no leading zeros, no "+", no "-0". Quantities are strings.
  Strings: escape only '"', '\', and U+0000..U+001F (as \b \t \n \f \r or \u00xx
  lowercase). Everything else is literal UTF-8.
  An entry is valid only if re-serialising it reproduces its bytes exactly.

PRIMITIVES
  decimal = [ "-" ] 1*DIGIT [ "." 1*DIGIT ], optionally prefixed "~" (estimate)
  address = segment ":" sequence, both >= 1
  hash    = "sha256:" 64 lowercase hex, or bare 64 lowercase hex where stated

TYPE BYTE
  byte = phase << 6 | code ; phase instant=0 begin=1 end=2 sample=3
  code = block << 3 | index

VERBS (code name), eight blocks of eight
  00 POWER_CHANGE 01 MODE_CHANGE 02 GATE 03 RESTORE 04 CALIBRATE 05 ASSIGN_CLASS 06 SUSPEND 07 RESUME
  08 GRANT 09 REVOKE 0A DELEGATE 0B EXPIRE 0C CREATE 0D BIND 0E ATTEST 0F VERIFY
  10 OPEN 11 CLOSE 12 JOIN 13 LEAVE 14 REMOVE 15 COMMIT 16 AMEND 17 DISPUTE
  18 SEND 19 RECEIVE 1A ACK 1B INVOICE 1C PAY 1D QUOTE 1E AGREE 1F COMPLETE
  20 ASSIGN 21 START 22 PROGRESS 23 FINISH 24 INSPECT 25 REPORT 26 EXPENSE 27 TRAVEL
  28 OBSERVE 29 NOTE 2A JOURNAL 2B CAPTURE 2C MEASURE 2D ESTIMATE 2E COMPARE 2F ALERT
  30 SCHEDULE 31 RESCHEDULE 32 CANCEL 33 REMIND 34 TRIGGER 35 RECUR 36 ATTEND 37 DELEGATE_EVENT
  38 QUERY 39 RESPOND 3A ACT 3B DEFER 3C LEARN 3D TEACH 3E RECOMMEND 3F AUTOMATE
  Names are unique; mapping to other systems is by code, never by name.

BOOTSTRAP VERBS (kernel meaning; required payload keys)
  CREATE  declare the thing named by object             kind
  BIND    attach; object "log" with pack adopts a pack   pack | to
  GRANT   give authority                                 to, may
  REVOKE  withdraw authority                             to, may
  AMEND   supersede an earlier entry, which remains      ref, reason
  OPEN    genesis or segment start                       kind ("genesis": log, root, clock, tz; "segment": prev)
  COMMIT  seal a segment or close a period               kind ("seal": n, sha256, chain; "close": book, upto, balances)
  ACK     receipt; a rejection carries "refused"         rec, digest
  Every entry carries "rec", the recorder. Genesis's rec is its first root.

SEALS
  A seal's sha256 covers every byte of its segment before the seal line,
  preamble included. chain = sha256(hex(previous chain) || hex(sha256)), the
  first previous chain being 64 zeros. A segment after a seal begins with
  OPEN kind=segment carrying prev = that chain.

CORE RULES
  1. Append only. Nothing written is ever changed or removed.
  2. An entry is valid if it is well formed, canonical, and its recorder's
     authority traces to a root named in genesis.
  3. An entry is read under the declarations in force at its position.
